  Let me explain the "callbacks can't ERROR out" constraint - it's a fundamental design issue in async I/O systems.

  ---
  The Completion Callback Problem

  The Core Issue

  In PostgreSQL's async I/O model, any backend can complete any other backend's I/O. This happens when:

  1. Backend A submits a read for buffer X
  2. Backend A goes off to do other work (the whole point of async)
  3. Backend B needs buffer X, waits on it
  4. Backend B ends up processing the completion (draining the io_uring CQ or checking AIO status)

  From README.md lines 180-194:
  Consider one backend performing readahead on a table, initiating IO for a
  number of buffers ahead of the current "scan position". If that backend then
  performs some operation that blocks, or even just is slow, the IO completion
  for the asynchronously initiated read may not be processed.

  This AIO implementation solves this problem by requiring that AIO methods
  either allow AIO completions to be processed by any backend in the system
  (e.g. io_uring), or to guarantee that AIO processing will happen even when the
  issuing backend is blocked (e.g. worker mode).

  Why Callbacks Can't Throw Errors

  From aio_internal.h lines 235-238:
  /*
   * NB: This is called in a critical section. Errors can be signalled by
   * the callback's return value, it's the responsibility of the IO's issuer
   * to react appropriately.
   */
  PgAioHandleCallbackComplete complete_shared;

  The problem:

  Backend A: SELECT * FROM huge_table;  (issues async reads)
             ↓
             Now doing unrelated work, holding locks

  Backend B: Needs same buffer, calls pgaio_wref_wait()
             ↓
             Drains completion queue, finds Backend A's IO failed (disk error)
             ↓
             ??? Can't ERROR here - wrong transaction context!
             ??? Can't ERROR here - might be in critical section!
             ??? Can't ERROR here - Backend A holds locks we might need!

  If Backend B throws ERROR when completing Backend A's I/O:
  - Wrong transaction - the error would abort Backend B's transaction, not A's
  - Critical section - completions run in START_CRIT_SECTION() (see method_io_uring.c:550-573)
  - Deadlock risk - Backend A might hold locks that error recovery needs

  PostgreSQL's Solution: Deferred Error Reporting

  From README.md lines 380-392:
  To allow to react to failing IOs the issuing backend can pass a pointer to a
  `PgAioReturn` in backend local memory. Before an AIO Handle is reused the
  `PgAioReturn` is filled with information about the result.

  The flow becomes:

  // Backend A (issuing)
  PgAioReturn ioret;
  PgAioHandle *ioh = pgaio_io_acquire(CurrentResourceOwner, &ioret);
  // ... submit IO ...
  pgaio_wref_wait(&iow);  // Block until complete

  // NOW check for errors (back in Backend A's context)
  if (ioret.result.status == PGAIO_RS_ERROR)
      pgaio_result_report(ioret.result, &ioret.target_data, ERROR);  // Safe to ERROR here

  The completion callback can only:
  1. Update shared state (buffer descriptors, etc.)
  2. Return a PgAioResult struct encoding the error
  3. Log at most a LOG level message (not ERROR)

  The issuing backend later calls pgaio_result_report() to actually raise the error.

  ---
  How This Affects TigerBeetle

  Looking at your freebsd.zig, you handle this similarly in handle_aio_completion() (lines 204-229):

  fn handle_aio_completion(self: *IO, completion: *Completion) void {
      // Remove from aio_pending queue
      self.aio_pending.remove(completion);

      // DON'T process the result here - just queue for callback
      completion.link = .{};
      self.completed.push(completion);  // Deferred to flush() loop
  }

  Then in the read callback (lines 660-704), the issuing context checks the result:

  fn on_complete(io: *IO, _completion: *Completion) void {
      const op = &_completion.operation.aio_read;

      if (op.submit_failed) {
          callback(..., error.AioSubmitFailed);  // Error reported in issuer's context
          return;
      }

      const err = c.aio_error(&op.aiocb);
      if (err != 0) {
          callback(..., error.AioError);  // Error reported in issuer's context
          return;
      }
      // ...
  }

  This is the same pattern - completion just queues the work, the actual error handling happens when the issuing code processes the completion.

  ---
  The Real Concern: Callback Complexity

  The deeper issue PostgreSQL grapples with (from README.md lines 305-330):

  Commonly several layers need to react to completion of an IO:
  - md.c needs to check if the IO outright failed or was shorter than needed
  - bufmgr.c needs to verify the page looks valid
  - bufmgr.c needs to update the BufferDesc state

  Upper layers should not need to know details of lower layers.
  Lower layers should not need to know about upper layers.

  PostgreSQL solves this with stacked callbacks (PGAIO_HANDLE_MAX_CALLBACKS = 4):

  // Layer 1: md.c registers callback to check raw IO result
  pgaio_io_register_callbacks(ioh, PGAIO_HCB_MD_READV, 0);

  // Layer 2: bufmgr.c registers callback to validate page checksum
  pgaio_io_register_callbacks(ioh, PGAIO_HCB_SHARED_BUFFER_READV, 0);

  Each callback transforms the result for the layer above. This is elegant but complex.

  TigerBeetle's simpler architecture (single-purpose database) likely doesn't need this layering - your callbacks are direct and purpose-specific.

  ---
  Summary

  The "callbacks can't ERROR" constraint exists because:
  ┌────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          Problem           │                            Why It Matters                             │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Wrong backend completes IO │ ERROR would abort wrong transaction                                   │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Critical sections          │ Can't throw during START_CRIT_SECTION                                 │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Lock ordering              │ Error recovery might deadlock                                         │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Memory allocation          │ Error formatting allocates; can't guarantee memory in completion path │
  └────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  Your TigerBeetle implementation handles this correctly - handle_aio_completion() just queues to completed, and errors are surfaced in the callback invoked by flush() in the original context.

