  TigerBeetle:                      PostgreSQL:
  ┌─────────────────┐               ┌─────────────────┐
  │  Single Process │               │   Postmaster    │
  │  ┌───────────┐  │               └────────┬────────┘
  │  │  kqueue   │  │                   fork │ fork
  │  │  AIO ops  │  │               ┌───────┴───────┐
  │  │  buffers  │  │               ▼               ▼
  │  └───────────┘  │         ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐
  └─────────────────┘         │ Backend A│    │ Backend B│
                              │ (fd=7)   │    │ (fd=7)   │ ← Different files!
                              └──────────┘    └──────────┘

  The same fd number in different backends refers to different files. This is why method_worker.c needs pgaio_io_reopen().

  ---
  Can We Share a kqueue Across Processes?

  Yes, actually. kqueue fds are inherited across fork() and refer to the same kernel object. Let me trace through how this would work:

  // Postmaster (before fork):
  int kq = kqueue();  // Creates kernel kqueue object, gets fd=5

  // After fork, Backend A:
  // fd=5 still points to the SAME kernel kqueue

  // After fork, Backend B:
  // fd=5 still points to the SAME kernel kqueue

  // Any process can call:
  kevent(kq, ...)  // All see the same events!

  This is exactly how PostgreSQL's io_uring implementation works - rings are created in postmaster and inherited.

  ---
  Proposed method_posix_aio.c Design

  Here's what it would look like, following the io_uring pattern:

  typedef struct PgAioPosixAioContext {
      LWLock      completion_lock;    // Same pattern as io_uring
      int         kqueue_fd;          // Created by postmaster, inherited
  } PgAioPosixAioContext;

  // Shared memory: one context + array of aiocb structs
  static PgAioPosixAioContext *pgaio_posix_context;
  static struct aiocb *pgaio_aiocbs;  // In shared memory!

  const IoMethodOps pgaio_posix_aio_ops = {
      .wait_on_fd_before_close = true,  // Same as io_uring

      .shmem_size = pgaio_posix_aio_shmem_size,
      .shmem_init = pgaio_posix_aio_shmem_init,
      .init_backend = NULL,  // Just inherit kqueue_fd

      .submit = pgaio_posix_aio_submit,
      .wait_one = pgaio_posix_aio_wait_one,
  };

  Submit Implementation

  static int
  pgaio_posix_aio_submit(uint16 num_staged_ios, PgAioHandle **staged_ios)
  {
      for (int i = 0; i < num_staged_ios; i++)
      {
          PgAioHandle *ioh = staged_ios[i];
          struct aiocb *cb = &pgaio_aiocbs[pgaio_io_get_id(ioh)];
          struct iovec *iov;

          memset(cb, 0, sizeof(*cb));

          pgaio_io_prepare_submit(ioh);

          // Set up aiocb
          cb->aio_fildes = ioh->op_data.read.fd;
          cb->aio_offset = ioh->op_data.read.offset;

          // Point to shared memory buffer (iovec is already in shmem)
          iov = &pgaio_ctl->iovecs[ioh->iovec_off];
          cb->aio_buf = iov->iov_base;
          cb->aio_nbytes = iov->iov_len;

          // SIGEV_KEVENT: deliver completion to shared kqueue
          cb->aio_sigevent.sigev_notify = SIGEV_KEVENT;
          cb->aio_sigevent.sigev_notify_kqueue = pgaio_posix_context->kqueue_fd;
          cb->aio_sigevent.sigev_value.sival_ptr = ioh;  // ioh is in shmem

          // Submit!
          if (ioh->op == PGAIO_OP_READV)
              aio_read(cb);
          else
              aio_write(cb);
      }

      return num_staged_ios;
  }

  Wait/Drain Implementation

  static void
  pgaio_posix_aio_wait_one(PgAioHandle *ioh, uint64 ref_generation)
  {
      PgAioHandleState state;

      LWLockAcquire(&pgaio_posix_context->completion_lock, LW_EXCLUSIVE);

      while (true)
      {
          // Check if already done (another backend completed it)
          if (pgaio_io_was_recycled(ioh, ref_generation, &state) ||
              state != PGAIO_HS_SUBMITTED)
              break;

          // Wait for completions
          struct kevent events[32];
          struct timespec ts = {0, 0};  // Non-blocking first

          int n = kevent(pgaio_posix_context->kqueue_fd,
                         NULL, 0, events, 32, &ts);

          if (n == 0)
          {
              // Nothing ready, do a blocking wait
              ts.tv_sec = 1;
              n = kevent(pgaio_posix_context->kqueue_fd,
                         NULL, 0, events, 32, &ts);
          }

          // Process completions
          START_CRIT_SECTION();
          for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
          {
              if (events[i].filter == EVFILT_AIO)
              {
                  PgAioHandle *completed_ioh = events[i].udata;
                  struct aiocb *cb = &pgaio_aiocbs[pgaio_io_get_id(completed_ioh)];

                  int err = aio_error(cb);
                  if (err == EINPROGRESS)
                      continue;  // Shouldn't happen with EVFILT_AIO

                  int result = (err == 0) ? aio_return(cb) : -err;
                  pgaio_io_process_completion(completed_ioh, result);
              }
          }
          END_CRIT_SECTION();
      }

      LWLockRelease(&pgaio_posix_context->completion_lock);
  }

  ---
  Why Hasn't This Been Done?

  Looking at the code and discussions, several reasons:

  1. Scatter/Gather Limitation

  From Thomas Munro's wiki notes:
  "Direct I/O cannot handle scatter/gather beyond single vectors"

  PostgreSQL's AIO uses readv/writev with multiple iovecs:

  // io_uring supports this natively:
  io_uring_prep_readv(sqe, fd, iov, iovcnt, offset);

  // FreeBSD AIO does NOT have aio_readv()!
  // Only: aio_read(aiocb)  -- single buffer

  This is a real limitation. PostgreSQL batches multiple buffers into one IO for efficiency. With posix_aio, you'd need one aio_read() per buffer, increasing syscall count.

  2. Vectored I/O Workaround Complexity

  To match io_uring's efficiency, you'd need:

  // Instead of one readv() for 16 pages:
  io_uring_prep_readv(sqe, fd, iov, 16, offset);  // 1 syscall

  // With posix_aio, either:
  // Option A: 16 separate aio_read() calls (16 syscalls, 16 completions)
  // Option B: Use lio_listio() for batching

  lio_listio() exists but has different semantics and may not integrate cleanly with SIGEV_KEVENT.

  3. io_uring Investment

  PostgreSQL invested heavily in io_uring (look at method_io_uring.c - 700+ lines). Linux is the dominant deployment platform. The worker fallback handles everything else "well enough."

  4. macOS Limitations

  From the wiki:
  "macOS's restrictive AIO limits"

  A posix_aio backend would need to handle both FreeBSD (good AIO) and macOS (limited AIO). This complicates the implementation.

