  The Real Blocker: Vectored I/O

  PostgreSQL's read_stream.c and buffer manager want to do:

  // Read 8 consecutive pages in one IO
  smgrstartreadv(ioh, smgr, fork, blkno, pages, 8);

  With io_uring: 1 syscall, 1 completion
  With posix_aio: 8 syscalls, 8 completions (or complex lio_listio handling)

  This isn't a dealbreaker, but it means posix_aio would be slower than worker mode for large sequential scans, which is PostgreSQL's bread and butter.

  ---
  What Would Make It Worth Doing?

  1. If io_uring remains blocked in enterprise environments (security concerns)
  2. If FreeBSD/ZFS becomes a target platform (your use case!)
  3. If someone implements aio_readv() for FreeBSD (kernel work)

  For TigerBeetle's use case (fixed-size blocks, known patterns), the single-buffer limitation doesn't matter as much. For PostgreSQL's general-purpose workloads, it's more significant.

